# Agent, models, tracing

## Agent (SPEC.md §5)
- Exactly five nodes, in this order: `select_samples` → `extract_evidence` → `tie_out`
  → `flag_exceptions` → `draft_workpaper`. No extra nodes, no loops, no tool-calling
  sub-agents. Ask before changing the graph.
- `tie_out` is deterministic Python. It never calls a model.
- Answer keys never leak into the agent. Only `auditlab/models/stub.py`, `evals/` and
  `feedback/` may read `answer_key.json`. Agent nodes, prompts and real-provider clients
  must not import or receive it. A test enforces this (import scan of `auditlab/agent/`).
- Each node reads and writes only its own keys on `AgentState` (SPEC.md §5.2).
  Every node output is a Pydantic model so scorers can grade it without parsing prose.
- Nodes receive a `ModelClient`, never a provider SDK directly. The graph must run
  end to end with `StubModel`.
- Prompts are versioned files: `auditlab/agent/prompts/<version>/<node>.md`, loaded by
  name. Never edit a committed prompt version; create the next one.

## Evidence and workpaper guard
- `extract_evidence` is quote-or-drop: every evidence field carries `doc_id`, `page_no`
  and a `quote` that is an exact substring of that page's normalized text. No quote,
  no field — record it in `dropped` with a reason.
- `draft_workpaper` writes prose with `[[ev:<evidence_id>]]` markers where amounts go.
  `auditlab/guard.py` rejects any bare currency amount or digit run in model prose and any
  marker that does not resolve. Guard failures are scored, not hidden.

## Model-swap layer (SPEC.md §6)
- One protocol, `ModelClient.complete(request: ModelRequest) -> ModelResponse`, returning
  parsed structured output, token counts, latency in ms, and the provider's model id.
- Providers: `stub`, `replay`, plus optional `openai`, `anthropic`, `gemini` behind
  extras. A provider module imports its SDK lazily inside the client, so a missing SDK
  only breaks that provider.
- Model ids, prompt versions and prices come from `config/models.toml`. Never hardcode a
  model id string in Python.
- The cache key is sha256 of (provider, model_id, prompt_version, node, canonical JSON of
  the input, run_index). `run_index` is in the key so N repeated runs are N real samples,
  not one cached answer reused.
- Stub models are oracle-plus-fault-injection and are labelled `simulated = true`.
  Fault profiles are named and act at a single node (SPEC.md §6.3) so the regression
  shows up at its source step.

## Tracing (SPEC.md §7)
- Every node run is an OpenTelemetry span with `gen_ai.*` attributes (system, request
  model, input/output tokens) plus `audit.case_id`, `audit.run_index`, `audit.node`.
  Spans go to `runs/<run_id>/spans.jsonl` via the JSONL exporter in `auditlab/telemetry.py`.
- Remote sinks are optional and independent: LangSmith (`LANGSMITH_TRACING=true` +
  `LANGSMITH_API_KEY`), Langfuse (`LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY` + `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY` +
  `LANGFUSE_HOST`), and any OTLP backend (`OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`). Each SDK is
  imported only when its env vars are set. The demo and tests never need one.
- When a remote sink is on, attach every `StepScore` to its trace there (LangSmith
  feedback, Langfuse score). Use the installed SDK's documented API; don't guess names.
- Store every enabled sink's ref on each step record; `trace_ref` is the first remote one,
  else the local span id.
